        "## 5. Gradient Clipping\n",
        "\n",
        "### Why do we need Gradient Clipping?\n",
        "\n",
        "During backpropagation, gradients are calculated to update the network's weights and biases. Sometimes gradients become excessively large, known as the **Exploding Gradients Problem**.\n",
        "\n",
        "This can cause:\n",
        "- Very large weight updates.\n",
        "- Unstable training and fluctuating loss.\n",
        "- Numerical overflow (`NaN` values).\n",
        "- Failure of the model to converge.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Gradient Clipping** limits the gradient magnitude before updating model parameters, helping stabilize training.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Gradient Descent Update\n",
        "\n",
        "The parameter update is:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\theta_{t+1} = \\theta_t - \\eta g_t\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Where:\n",
        "- $\\theta_t$ = Model parameters at step $t$.\n",
        "- $\\eta$ = Learning rate.\n",
        "- $g_t = \\nabla_\\theta L(\\theta_t)$ = Gradient of the loss.\n",
        "\n",
        "Large gradients can result in excessively large parameter updates.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Gradient Clipping by Global Norm\n",
        "\n",
        "For a gradient vector:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "g = [g_1, g_2, \\ldots, g_n]\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Its L2 norm is:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\|g\\|_2 = \\sqrt{\\sum_{i=1}^{n} g_i^2}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Given a clipping threshold $c$, the clipped gradient is:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "g_{\\text{clipped}} =\n",
        "\\begin{cases}\n",
        "g, & \\|g\\|_2 \\leq c \\\\\n",
        "\\frac{c}{\\|g\\|_2}g, & \\|g\\|_2 > c\n",
        "\\end{cases}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "- If the gradient norm is within the threshold, it remains unchanged.\n",
        "- If it exceeds the threshold, the gradient is scaled down to the threshold.\n",
        "- Its direction is preserved, but its magnitude is reduced.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Example\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose $g = [3,4]$ and the clipping threshold is $c=2$.\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\|g\\|_2 = \\sqrt{3^2+4^2}=5\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Since $5 > 2$:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "g_{\\text{clipped}} = \\frac{2}{5}[3,4] = [1.2,1.6]\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The gradient norm is reduced from 5 to 2.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Implementation in TensorFlow/Keras\n",
        "\n",
        "We use `global_clipnorm=1.0` in the Adam optimizer to limit the global gradient norm to 1.0.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Note:** `clipnorm` clips each variable's gradient separately, whereas `global_clipnorm` clips using the combined global norm. We use `global_clipnorm` because it matches the formula explained above.\n",
        "\n",
        "Gradient clipping improves optimization stability but does not directly prevent overfitting."
      ]
