      "source": [
        "### Build a Multi-Output Model\n",
        "\n",
        "The input to the network is an MNIST image of shape $28 \\times 28$.\n",
        "\n",
        "The model consists of a shared feature-extraction layer followed by two separate output branches.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 1. Model Architecture\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **`Flatten()`**: Converts the $28 \\times 28$ image into a one-dimensional vector containing $784$ pixel values.\n",
        "\n",
        "2. **`Dense(128, activation=\"relu\")`**: Learns $128$ useful features from the image. These features are shared by both prediction tasks.\n",
        "\n",
        "3. **Two output layers:** The network branches into two separate output layers, each responsible for a different classification task.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 2. Why Does the Model Have Two Outputs?\n",
        "\n",
        "The model performs **two classification tasks simultaneously** using the same input image.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Output 1 – Digit Classification**\n",
        "\n",
        "- Architecture: `Dense(10, activation=\"softmax\")`\n",
        "- MNIST contains 10 possible digit classes: $0,1,2,\\ldots,9$.\n",
        "- Therefore, we use **10 neurons**, one for each digit.\n",
        "- Softmax converts the output scores into probabilities whose sum is $1$.\n",
        "\n",
        "For example:\n",
        "\n",
        "`[0.01, 0.02, 0.90, 0.01, ...]`\n",
        "\n",
        "The highest probability is at index $2$, so the predicted digit is **2**.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Output 2 – Parity Classification**\n",
        "\n",
        "- Architecture: `Dense(1, activation=\"sigmoid\")`\n",
        "- The task is to classify the digit as either **even or odd**.\n",
        "- Since there are only two classes, we use **one output neuron**.\n",
        "- The sigmoid activation produces a probability between $0$ and $1$.\n",
        "\n",
        "Interpretation:\n",
        "\n",
        "- Output $< 0.5$: Even\n",
        "- Output $\\geq 0.5$: Odd\n",
        "\n",
        "For example, an output of $0.85$ indicates an odd digit.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 3. Why Not Use Two Separate Models?\n",
        "\n",
        "Both tasks require similar image features. Instead of learning these features independently, the model learns them once in the shared layer and uses them for both tasks.\n",
        "\n",
        "This approach is called **Multi-Task Learning**.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 4. Final Architecture Summary\n",
        "\n",
        "| Component | Output Shape | Purpose |\n",
        "|---|---|---|\n",
        "| Input | $28 \\times 28$ | MNIST image |\n",
        "| Flatten | $784$ | Converts image into a vector |\n",
        "| Shared Dense | $128$ | Feature extraction |\n",
        "| Digit Output | $10$ | Predicts digits $0-9$ |\n",
        "| Parity Output | $1$ | Predicts even or odd |\n",
        "\n",
        "**Important:** The model has two output tensors corresponding to two tasks, but a total of **11 output neurons** ($10+1$).\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus, a single neural network performs two related classification tasks through shared feature learning."
      ],
