        "## 7. Key Takeaways\n",
        "\n",
        "| Technique | Main purpose | How it works |\n",
        "|---|---|---|\n",
        "| Dropout | Reduce overfitting | Randomly disables neurons during training |\n",
        "| Gradient Clipping | Stabilize optimization | Limits gradient magnitude |\n",
        "| Early Stopping | Avoid unnecessary training and overfitting | Stops when validation performance stops improving |"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Final observations\n",
        "- Dropout discourages the network from relying too heavily on specific neurons. The dropout mask changes during training, and all neurons are used during inference.\n",
        "- Gradient clipping prevents excessively large gradients from causing unstable parameter updates. In this notebook, global_clipnorm=1.0 limits the global L2 norm of the gradients to 1.0.\n",
        "- Early stopping monitors validation loss, stops when it does not improve for the chosen patience, and restores the weights from the best epoch.\n",
        "Important distinction\n",
        "Gradient clipping is not exactly the same type of regularization as Dropout. Dropout changes the network's behavior during training to help generalization, while gradient clipping controls optimization stability. Early stopping limits how long the model continues fitting the training data.\n",
        "\n",
        "Conclusion: These techniques address different training challenges. They can be useful individually or together, depending on the model and dataset. The numerical results may vary with random initialization, software versions and hardware."
